<p>The asymmetry is deliberate. Parliament built a wide social-justice net for wives, children and parents, and a narrower, reciprocal equity inside the Hindu Marriage Act alone. A husband\'s claim therefore lives and dies within a pending or concluded HMA proceeding.</p>

<h2>The real test: incapacity, not idleness</h2>
<p>The text of Section 24 conditions relief on the claimant having "no independent income sufficient for his or her support". For husbands, courts have grafted onto this a consistent gloss: the provision protects those who <em>cannot</em> earn, not those who <em>will not</em>.</p>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old"><h4>Claims that fail</h4><p>An able-bodied, educated husband citing unemployment alone; income concealed or understated; a claim mounted as leverage against the wife\'s own maintenance application; reliance on the wife\'s salary as a substitute for effort.</p></div>
  <div class="arrow">→</div>
  <div class="col new"><h4>Claims with substance</h4><p>Serious illness or disability destroying earning capacity, proved medically; a husband who demonstrably ran the household while the wife built her career; age and labour-market reality that make re-employment implausible; expenses of the litigation where the wife\'s means vastly exceed his.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>How quantum is approached</h2>
<p>Where a husband crosses the threshold, quantum follows the ordinary discipline of maintenance law as consolidated in <em>Rajnesh v. Neha</em>: affidavits of disclosure from both sides, and an assessment balancing the status of the parties, reasonable needs, actual incomes and liabilities, and the standard of living during the marriage. Two features deserve emphasis in husband-claims. First, litigation expenses under Section 24 are often the more realistic component — courts readily equalise the ability to contest proceedings even where they hesitate over monthly support. Second, any award remains variable: Section 25(2) permits modification on a change of circumstances, so a husband who regains earning capacity can expect the award to shrink or end.</p>

<h2>Strategic notes for both sides</h2>
<div class="check">
  <ul>
    <li><strong>For husbands considering a claim:</strong> plead incapacity with particulars — medical records, employment history, the family arrangement that curtailed your earning — and file a complete assets affidavit before being asked. A thin claim invites costs and colours the entire matrimonial case.</li>
    <li><strong>For wives resisting a claim:</strong> the claimant\'s qualifications, past income, and capacity to earn are the pressure points; interrogatories and bank summons under the disclosure regime expose unexplained lifestyles.</li>
    <li><strong>For both:</strong> Section 24 applications are meant to be decided swiftly, on affidavits, without becoming a trial within the trial — prolixity hurts the party that benefits from delay.</li>
    <li><strong>Remember the one-way statutes:</strong> no tactical creativity makes a husband eligible under Section 144 BNSS, HAMA or the DV Act; a claim filed there is stillborn.</li>
  </ul>
</div>
<p>Gender-neutral maintenance inside the HMA is a narrow equity, honestly available but sternly policed. It exists for the spouse genuinely unable to sustain the litigation or himself — and for no one else.</p>
<div class="note"><p>This article is for general information only and is not legal advice. Outcomes depend on the evidence of income, capacity and conduct in each case.</p></div>
